WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.601
-Welcome back.
It is always great to see you.

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.534
Michelle, congratulations,
by the way.

00:00:04.534 --> 00:00:05.801
Yesterday,
you just got nominated

00:00:05.801 --> 00:00:07.601
for a Critic's Choice Award.
-I know.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's a big deal.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.834
♪♪

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:14.200
-I know. It's such an
illustrious group of actresses.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.501
It's such an honor.

00:00:15.501 --> 00:00:19.234
-You crushed it as Betty Ford
in "The First Lady."

00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:23.167
Yeah, she was amazing.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.434
I was reading up
a little bit about you,

00:00:24.434 --> 00:00:26.601
and I saw something
I thought was interesting.

00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:29.667
That when you get a role
or a job, that you either

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:31.300
try to get out of it
or try to get fired from it.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.100
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.367
-Is that true?
-I mean, sometimes the only way

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.300
to get out is to get fired.
-But that's true?

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.634
-It's just all --
It's just --

00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:41.934
My hands are sweating
as we're even discussing this.

00:00:41.934 --> 00:00:44.067
I get performance anxiety.
-You do?

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:46.300
-Yeah, and I do.
And I get --

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.601
You know, people are always
so surprised that an actor

00:00:48.601 --> 00:00:51.400
would have performance anxiety,
but...

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.601
-But you do.
-Yeah.

00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:54.033
And I guess they say --
they do say, though,

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:57.167
when that stops,
you should really hang it up.

00:00:57.167 --> 00:00:58.868
-Oh.
-That it's sort of part of

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:01.400
the juice that kind of
gets us going.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.634
-Yeah.
Every night I feel like --

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.033
"Yeah, I'm not coming out."
-Yeah?

00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:07.934
[ Laughter ]

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.467
And then I just keep coming out,
yeah.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:10.901
-You keep coming out.
-I keep coming out.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:13.434
But every movie?
"Scarface?" Everything?

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:16.400
"Batman Returns"?
-No, no.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.234
I didn't, because I was
obsessed with Catwoman

00:01:20.234 --> 00:01:22.000
since I was a little girl.
-Yeah.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:26.667
-Yeah.
And, actually,

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.400
someone was cast before me --

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.167
Annette Benning,
who's wonderful.

00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:32.033
-Wow.
-And then she got pregnant.

00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:33.133
Awesome.
-Yeah.

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:35.334
-And then I got the part!
I got the part!

00:01:35.334 --> 00:01:37.067
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Wow.

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:39.067
You nailed it.

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:42.701
And, you know, congrats on
"The Ant-Man" movies, as well.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.634
Do you like
doing superhero movies?

00:01:44.634 --> 00:01:46.033
I think you're
pretty good in them.

00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.934
-Yeah, I mean, they're kind --
look, they're a mixed bag.

00:01:48.934 --> 00:01:52.868
You know, like, the outfits
are always super uncomfortable,

00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:55.667
and you forget it's like
childbirth a little bit.

00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:57.367
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.367 --> 00:02:02.968
But it's -- you know, "Ant-Man"
is such a fun one to work on.

00:02:02.968 --> 00:02:05.534
It's such
a great group of people.

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:08.434
-Great cast.
-And funny and smart.

00:02:08.434 --> 00:02:09.834
And it was really fun,
this last one,

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:12.300
because, first one, I wasn't
really in it that much.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.100
I sort of showed up
for a couple weeks

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.334
and did my bit and then left.

00:02:16.334 --> 00:02:19.868
And I didn't really honestly
get to know them.

00:02:19.868 --> 00:02:22.234
But this time,
I was there for the long run.

00:02:22.234 --> 00:02:24.767
-Yeah.
Paul Rudd said that

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:27.234
everyone freaked out
at "The Avengers: Endgame,"

00:02:27.234 --> 00:02:29.267
the final scene,
when you walked on set.

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:30.901
And they were all sitting there,
like, "Oh, my God.

00:02:30.901 --> 00:02:32.267
Michelle Pfeiffer."
-No.

00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:34.834
-Yeah, that is definitely true.
He told me, he said that.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:38.234
-Well, I wasn't aware of that.
I was freaking out.

00:02:38.234 --> 00:02:40.133
-You were trying
to get out of it.

00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:43.801
You were trying to get fired
from "Avengers: Endgame."

00:02:43.801 --> 00:02:46.200
Like, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.367
Do you ever get excited
about meeting anyone?

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:49.834
-I do.

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:54.200
Actually, I was really excited
to work with Jonathan Majors.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.734
He's in "Ant-Man,"
and we have --

00:02:56.734 --> 00:02:58.534
A lot of our scenes
are together.

00:02:58.534 --> 00:03:01.934
And, you know, it's an
interesting, this one is.

00:03:01.934 --> 00:03:03.400
I don't know
what I'm allowed to say.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.267
-Yeah. I know, you're really
not allowed to say much.

00:03:05.267 --> 00:03:06.968
-They give you a whole list
of what you can't say

00:03:06.968 --> 00:03:09.534
but not anything
what you can say, so...

00:03:09.534 --> 00:03:11.901
-Next time you come on,
I want you to bring that list.

00:03:11.901 --> 00:03:13.300
[ Laughter ]
I want to read what you --

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:14.634
-And I'll just read it.

00:03:14.634 --> 00:03:16.501
-"Here's exactly what
I can't say about the movie."

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:19.133
-Yeah.
-Also, your giant movie career.

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:21.534
You also have
this amazing fragrance line.

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:23.467
Henry Rose.
-I do.

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:25.067
-Congratulations on this.
-Oh, and you happen to have it

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:26.200
back there.
-Oh, yes, I do.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:27.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:29.734
And I want to talk about this.

00:03:29.734 --> 00:03:32.133
'Cause I do like
the packaging, as well.

00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:33.534
The packaging is very cool.

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:36.100
Can you see -- You can see
"Henry Rose" through the bottle.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:37.133
-Yeah.
-Can you see that?

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:39.267
-And how did this come about?

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:42.133
When did this start taking off?
-Well, it started --

00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:47.100
Really, the genesis of it
was really around 25 years ago

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.267
when I became a new mom.

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.367
And I started looking
at the world

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:53.567
through my children's eyes,
like a lot of people do.

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:55.968
And I started paying
closer attention to

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:58.934
what I was exposing them to
and looking at the ingredients

00:03:58.934 --> 00:04:02.234
and the things we were putting
on our bodies.

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:06.534
And -- And, you know,
what I discovered is that

00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:09.801
fragrance was completely
non-transparent.

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:13.501
It was really the last black box
of transparency.

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:16.734
And so I stopped wearing
fragrance for 10 years.

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:20.667
And I love, obviously --
I love fragrance.

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:23.667
And, um...

00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:25.868
-Was it difficult to get off
the ground, or were you like,

00:04:25.868 --> 00:04:27.801
"I'm Michelle Pfeiffer,
so let's just do this"?

00:04:27.801 --> 00:04:29.167
[ Laughter ]

00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:32.334
-No, no.
It was -- I --

00:04:32.334 --> 00:04:35.234
So, about 15 years ago, I --

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:38.334
And after trying all kinds of
concoctions on the market,

00:04:38.334 --> 00:04:40.367
I really missed that sort of
fine fragrance.

00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:45.968
And I decided, "Okay. Well,
I'm gonna see if I can do this.

00:04:45.968 --> 00:04:48.067
There must be other people
looking for this product."

00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:49.834
And nobody really understood it.

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:52.901
And I think I was just
a little too ahead of the curve.

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:55.834
And so, I --
It's a good thing I'm good

00:04:55.834 --> 00:05:00.601
with rejection because,
you know, I'd go out there

00:05:00.601 --> 00:05:03.501
and make a go of it
and get rejected,

00:05:03.501 --> 00:05:07.200
and I'd, you know,
go with my tail between my legs,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.534
and I'd shelve it for a while,
and then I'd bring it back.

00:05:09.534 --> 00:05:11.801
-You kept bringing it back.
-And try again.

00:05:11.801 --> 00:05:13.767
And then I'd shelve it
for two years.

00:05:13.767 --> 00:05:17.400
And then, finally,
about five years ago,

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.767
I thought, "Okay."
It was New Year's Day.

00:05:18.767 --> 00:05:20.434
I remember.
I said, "Okay.

00:05:20.434 --> 00:05:23.534
What do you want to do
this year?"

00:05:23.534 --> 00:05:26.033
"I'm gonna give that fragrance
thing one more try."

00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:31.167
-Yes!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:35.000
-So, anyway, 2019 --

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.067
So, I finally found --
I found --

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:41.267
I.F.F. was a great partner.

00:05:41.267 --> 00:05:44.033
I went directly to -- I bypassed
the cosmetic industry.

00:05:44.033 --> 00:05:46.167
I went directly
to the perfume house

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:48.667
and started working
with perfumers.

00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:53.367
And in 2019, we launched
with five transformative,

00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:57.634
100% transparent fragrances

00:05:57.634 --> 00:06:03.367
that really, you know,
disrupted the fragrance market.

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:07.400
And we really
created a new category.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.400
And so, we are the very first
genderless line of

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.801
luxury fine fragrances
to have earned

00:06:14.801 --> 00:06:17.868
the safety stamp of approval.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:17.868 --> 00:06:19.934
-It's amazing that you did that.
-Yeah.

00:06:19.934 --> 00:06:24.701
We launched
just direct to consumer.

00:06:24.701 --> 00:06:27.934
And this year,
we've moved into retail.

00:06:27.934 --> 00:06:30.567
And people
can go on the website

00:06:30.567 --> 00:06:34.200
and use the location finder
to find a store near you.

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.667
-Michelle, that's massive.
That's so cool.

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:37.033
-I know, it's very cool.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:37.033 --> 00:06:39.701
-Yeah, I love that.

00:06:39.701 --> 00:06:42.667
Do you find,
now that you're doing this now,

00:06:42.667 --> 00:06:47.467
do you have a heightened
sense of smell?

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:49.000
-Well...
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:51.067
Little fun fact.
-Yeah.

00:06:51.067 --> 00:06:54.167
-I was --
I was at the E.N.T., you know...

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:55.901
-Yeah.
-...getting probed, which is

00:06:55.901 --> 00:06:58.100
a really nice experience.
-Ear, nose, and throat, yeah.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:02.767
-Yeah, and he's looking up --
you know, looking up my nostril,

00:07:02.767 --> 00:07:04.033
and he says...
-That's what they do.

00:07:04.033 --> 00:07:07.033
-He goes, "Oh. Interesting."

00:07:07.033 --> 00:07:08.133
[ Laughter ]

00:07:08.133 --> 00:07:10.400
And I said, "What?
What's interesting?"

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:16.868
He goes, "Oh, well,
you have an organ

00:07:16.868 --> 00:07:19.534
in your nose..."
-What?

00:07:19.534 --> 00:07:22.701
-"...that people
don't usually have."

00:07:22.701 --> 00:07:26.534
It's like an organ, right?
-I don't know, yeah.

00:07:26.534 --> 00:07:28.167
[ Laughter ]

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:29.601
I don't know what I would do.

00:07:29.601 --> 00:07:34.234
-He said it's called
the vomeronasal organ.

00:07:34.234 --> 00:07:37.601
And you find it in mammals,
in animals.

00:07:37.601 --> 00:07:41.400
And they really use it

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:47.701
to detect pheromones and mate.

00:07:47.701 --> 00:07:48.834
-Wow!

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:52.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:52.634 --> 00:07:53.934
Wow.
Cool.

00:07:53.934 --> 00:07:56.400
-And I have this.
-You have it?

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.334
-So, I have sort of have
an animalistic

00:07:58.334 --> 00:08:01.033
kind of sense of smell, I guess.

00:08:01.033 --> 00:08:02.934
-This is perfect then!
See?

00:08:02.934 --> 00:08:04.801
-Who knew?
-Who knew?

00:08:04.801 --> 00:08:06.367
Well, I thought maybe we would
put it to the test

00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:08.734
and play a fun game
when we come back.

00:08:08.734 --> 00:08:11.133
Are you down with that?
-Yeah, I'm down with that.

00:08:11.133 --> 00:08:14.000
But you've practiced that game
a lot, and I haven't.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.801
-I don't practice anything.

00:08:15.801 --> 00:08:16.901
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:16.901 --> 00:08:19.300
I have no idea.
-Yeah, but you live here.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:20.868
-Are you looking for --
I do live here.

00:08:20.868 --> 00:08:23.067
[ Laughter ]
I sleep under this desk.

00:08:23.067 --> 00:08:24.534
-I'm looking for a scent --

00:08:24.534 --> 00:08:25.634
-Are you looking
for a scent for me?

00:08:25.634 --> 00:08:27.434
-Yeah, I want to try it on you.
This one.

00:08:27.434 --> 00:08:30.100
-How do I put it on --
Can I ask how I -- How do I --

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:31.734
-You want to do
the dolphin thing?

00:08:31.734 --> 00:08:34.234
-Yeah.
-All right.

00:08:34.234 --> 00:08:35.801
-I kind of dive into it.

00:08:35.801 --> 00:08:37.868
You spray it, and I'll
dolphin my way through it.

00:08:37.868 --> 00:08:39.033
-Okay. You ready?
-Yeah.

00:08:39.033 --> 00:08:40.868
-Okay.

00:08:40.868 --> 00:08:42.534
Wait.

00:08:42.534 --> 00:08:44.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:44.601 --> 00:08:46.100
-Wow!

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:48.901
Ooh, that's fun!
Yeah!

00:08:48.901 --> 00:08:50.968
Oh, I'm feeling it!
Now I got it now, too!

00:08:50.968 --> 00:08:53.033
I love it!
Michelle Pfeiffer and I

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:54.968
are playing a new game
after the break! Stick around!

